# UAM IDS — Server Setup Guide

## What This Server Does

Think of this server as a **security control room**. Your agents (installed on employee computers) send data every 30 seconds. This server:

1. **Receives** all that data
2. **Stores** it in a database
3. **Analyses** patterns over time (the correlation engine)
4. **Raises alerts** when it finds suspicious behaviour (email/Slack/webhook)

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `server.py` | Main server — receives data from agents, answers API calls |
| `correlator.py` | The "detective" — looks for multi-signal patterns over time |
| `alerter.py` | Sends email/Slack/webhook alerts to your security team |
| `models.py` | Database structure (tables, columns) |
| `config.py` | All settings — read from environment variables |
| `requirements.txt` | Python packages needed |

---

## Requirements

- Python 3.10 or newer
- A server (Windows Server, Ubuntu, or any cloud VM)
- PostgreSQL 14+ (or SQLite for small setups)

---

## Quick Start (SQLite — easiest, for testing)

```bash
# 1. Install Python packages
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 2. Set minimum required environment variables
set UAM_AGENT_APIKEY=MyAgentKey123        # Windows
set UAM_ANALYST_APIKEY=MyAnalystKey456
set UAM_HMAC_SECRET=MyHmacSecret789
set UAM_SECRET_KEY=MyFlaskSecret000

# Linux/macOS:
export UAM_AGENT_APIKEY=MyAgentKey123
export UAM_ANALYST_APIKEY=MyAnalystKey456
export UAM_HMAC_SECRET=MyHmacSecret789
export UAM_SECRET_KEY=MyFlaskSecret000

# 3. Start the server
python server.py
```

The server will start on http://0.0.0.0:5000 and create a local file `uam_ids.db` automatically.

---

## Production Setup (PostgreSQL)

### Step 1 — Install PostgreSQL

Ubuntu:
```bash
sudo apt install postgresql postgresql-contrib
sudo systemctl start postgresql
sudo systemctl enable postgresql
```

Windows: Download from https://www.postgresql.org/download/windows/

### Step 2 — Create database

```bash
sudo -u postgres psql
CREATE DATABASE uam_ids;
CREATE USER uam_user WITH PASSWORD 'StrongPassword123';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE uam_ids TO uam_user;
\q
```

### Step 3 — Set environment variables

Create a file called `.env` and load it before starting:

```env
UAM_DB_URL=postgresql://uam_user:StrongPassword123@localhost:5432/uam_ids
UAM_HOST=0.0.0.0
UAM_PORT=5000
UAM_SECRET_KEY=ChangeThisToSomethingRandom
UAM_AGENT_APIKEY=ChangeThisAgentKey
UAM_ANALYST_APIKEY=ChangeThisAnalystKey
UAM_HMAC_SECRET=ChangeThisHmacSecret

# Email alerts (Gmail example)
UAM_EMAIL_ENABLED=true
UAM_SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
UAM_SMTP_PORT=587
UAM_SMTP_USER=your-email@gmail.com
UAM_SMTP_PASS=your-app-password
UAM_SMTP_FROM=uam-ids@yourcompany.com
UAM_ALERT_EMAIL_TO=security@yourcompany.com

# Slack alerts (optional)
UAM_SLACK_ENABLED=false
UAM_SLACK_WEBHOOK=https://hooks.slack.com/services/YOUR/WEBHOOK/URL

# Alert thresholds
UAM_ALERT_MIN_SEVERITY=HIGH
UAM_SCORE_CRITICAL=85
UAM_SCORE_HIGH=70
UAM_SCORE_MEDIUM=50
```

Load and run:
```bash
# Linux
export $(cat .env | xargs)
python server.py

# Or use python-dotenv:
pip install python-dotenv
```

### Step 4 — Run as a service (Linux systemd)

Create `/etc/systemd/system/uam-ids.service`:

```ini
[Unit]
Description=UAM IDS Server
After=network.target postgresql.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=ubuntu
WorkingDirectory=/opt/uam_server
EnvironmentFile=/opt/uam_server/.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/uam_server/server.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=5

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Enable and start:
```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable uam-ids
sudo systemctl start uam-ids
sudo systemctl status uam-ids
```

---

## Connecting Agents

On each monitored computer, set these environment variables before running the agent:

```
AGENT_SERVER=http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:5000/api/logs
AGENT_APIKEY=ChangeThisAgentKey       ← must match UAM_AGENT_APIKEY
AGENT_SECRET=ChangeThisHmacSecret     ← must match UAM_HMAC_SECRET
```

---

## Analyst REST API

All analyst endpoints require the header: `X-ANALYST-KEY: <your_analyst_key>`

### Dashboard summary
```
GET /api/analyst/summary
```
Returns: total agents, active agents, open incidents, payloads in last 24h, top risky agents.

### List agents
```
GET /api/analyst/agents?page=1&limit=50&search=username
```

### Get agent detail + risk trend
```
GET /api/analyst/agents/<agent_id>
```

### Get agent payloads (raw data)
```
GET /api/analyst/agents/<agent_id>/payloads?min_score=50&page=1
```

### List incidents
```
GET /api/analyst/incidents?status=OPEN&severity=HIGH
```
Status options: `OPEN`, `INVESTIGATING`, `CLOSED`, `FALSE_POSITIVE`
Severity options: `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `CRITICAL`

### Update incident (change status, add notes)
```
PATCH /api/analyst/incidents/<id>
Body: { "status": "INVESTIGATING", "analyst_notes": "Reviewed — user on notice period." }
```

### Watchlist — add user for extra scrutiny
```
POST /api/analyst/watchlist
Body: { "username": "jsmith", "reason": "On performance plan", "score_boost": 25 }
```

### Flag statistics (most common alerts)
```
GET /api/analyst/stats/flags?hours=24
```

### Risk timeline
```
GET /api/analyst/stats/risk_timeline?hours=24&agent_id=<optional>
```

### Manually trigger correlation
```
POST /api/analyst/correlate_now
```

---

## Correlation Rules (Built-in)

The correlation engine has 13 built-in rules. Each rule requires multiple signals before raising an incident:

| Rule | Severity | What It Detects |
|---|---|---|
| R01 | CRITICAL | Sensitive file copied to USB + network upload |
| R02 | CRITICAL | Impossible travel (two locations simultaneously) |
| R03 | CRITICAL | C2 beaconing + credential theft |
| R04 | HIGH | DNS tunneling data exfiltration |
| R05 | HIGH | Bulk email exfiltration after hours |
| R06 | HIGH | Privilege escalation + hacking tools |
| R07 | MEDIUM | Repeated off-hours access (slow burn) |
| R08 | HIGH | Large personal cloud upload |
| R09 | HIGH | Credential theft attempt |
| R10 | MEDIUM | Mass printing after hours |
| R11 | HIGH | Bot/automated activity with large upload |
| R12 | CRITICAL | Known malicious file detected |
| R13 | MEDIUM | VPN after hours + large upload |

---

## Risk Scoring

Every payload from an agent includes a **risk score (0–100)**. The server then adds a **correlation bonus** when multiple signals combine:

- Agent score 85+ → CRITICAL
- Agent score 70+ → HIGH  
- Agent score 50+ → MEDIUM
- Agent score 30+ → LOW

Scores **decay** when an agent has clean check-ins (85% per 4 hours by default). This prevents old incidents from staying open forever.

---

## Alert Channels

| Channel | Config variable | What to set |
|---|---|---|
| Email | UAM_EMAIL_ENABLED=true | SMTP settings |
| Slack | UAM_SLACK_ENABLED=true | Webhook URL |
| Webhook | UAM_WEBHOOK_ENABLED=true | URL (Teams, SIEM, etc.) |

Only incidents at or above `UAM_ALERT_MIN_SEVERITY` (default: HIGH) trigger alerts.

---

## Firewall / Network

The server listens on port **5000** by default. You need to:
- Allow inbound TCP 5000 from agent machines
- Block port 5000 from the internet (internal only)
- Or put Nginx/Apache in front as a reverse proxy for HTTPS

```bash
# Ubuntu firewall example
sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 5000  # allow your office network
sudo ufw deny 5000                                      # block everything else
```

---

## Troubleshooting

**Server won't start:**
- Check Python version: `python --version` (need 3.10+)
- Check all packages installed: `pip install -r requirements.txt`
- Check DB URL is correct

**Agents not connecting:**
- Verify `AGENT_SERVER` points to correct IP and port
- Verify `AGENT_APIKEY` matches `UAM_AGENT_APIKEY`
- Check firewall allows port 5000

**No incidents being created:**
- Check correlator is running: look for "Correlation run" in `uam_server.log`
- Agents may not have enough data yet (need a few minutes of check-ins)
- Risk scores may be below thresholds — check `/api/analyst/agents`

**Emails not sending:**
- For Gmail: use an App Password (not your regular password)
- Enable 2FA on Gmail first, then create App Password at myaccount.google.com
